Installation

The following sections describe the MS-DOS* and UNIX** installation for
the DSP1600 SGS Tools.

MS-DOS Installation

The MS-DOS distribution comes on one 3.5 inch floppy disk.  The software runs
on 386 or better PC with MS-DOS 4.0 or later and at least 2 megabytes of
extended memory.

The installation of the DSP1600 SGS tools is a two step procedure.
First, the installation routine copies the necessary software from the
distribution diskettes onto your hard drive.  Second, the installation
routine creates a file named NEW-VARS.BAT containing the environment
variables.  If the user allows the default, the installation
automatically adds these variables to the AUTOEXEC.BAT file.  To
perform the installation, follow the steps below.

     1)  Place the SGS disk marked "DSP1600 Family SGS" in the floppy drive
         of the host PC.
     2)  Enter "a:install" at the MS-DOS prompt (this assumes the diskette
         is in drive a:).
     3)  Answer the questions presented by the install routine.

Before copying the files to the hard drive, the install routine
prompts the user for the distribution diskette drive, the boot drive,
and the installation directory. If the installation directory contains
a previous release of the tools, the install routine will warn and
allow the user to quit the process. The user may then start the install
procedure again, specifying a new directory.  For more details on the
install procedure, please consult the DSP16XX Support Tools Manual.

It is VERY IMPORTANT that the PATH variable be properly setup as described
in NEW-VARS.BAT.  If an older version of as1600.exe is found first in the
PATH the remaining SGS tools will not be able to locate the Phar Lap DOS
extender embedded in as1600.exe (see New Features as1600 below.)


UNIX Installation

The UNIX distribution comes on one quarter inch data cartridge or one
3.5 inch floppy disk.  The software runs on a Sparc*** processor running
SunOS 4.1.3.

The UNIX installation of the DSP1600 SGS tools includes two steps.
First, the user copies the necessary software from the distribution
tape onto the hard drive.  Second, the user must modify the .profile
or .login to include the required environment variables.  To perform
the installation, follow the steps below.

     1)  Place the tape in the tape drive of the host system.
     2)  Enter "tar xvf /dev/rst0" at the prompt.
     3)  Edit .profile or .login files and add the following lines (m2_12ka.if
         can be any valid ifile):

     If you run the sh(1) or ksh(1) shell, the lines are
          LIB1600=<the tools directory>
          IFILE=$LIB1600/m2_12ka.if
          PATH=$PATH:$LIB1600
          export LIB1600 IFILE PATH

     If you run the csh (1), the lines are
          setenv LIB1600 <the tools directory>
          setenv IFILE $LIB1600/m2_12ka.if
          setenv path=($path $LIB1600)



