Description

The 3.2.1 release of the DSP1600 SGS Tools fixes exceptions and adds
enhancements to the last production release.  The DSP1600 family SGS Tools
have been separated from the DSP16XX-ST tools in the 3.2.1 release.



New Features
as1600

1.Support for extended DOS memory - All of the tools in this package now
  make use of the available extended memory in the user's PC.  The memory
  limitations which the earlier versions of these tools imposed have been
  lifted.  However, even though the new tools use extended memory, it is
  recommended that at least 400K of DOS memory be available. The code that
  allows execution in the extended DOS environment (DOS extender) is
  as1600.exe.  All executable tools in this release use the embedded DOS
  extender code in as1600.exe.  For execution of the support tools, the user
  needs to have the location of as1600.exe in the PATH.  If the required
  tool with the embedded extender code is not in the PATH, an error message
  similar to "Stub ldr err 7: DOS-Extender file as1600.exe not found" is
  issued.  Also, if an older version of the support tools is found in the
  PATH before as1600.exe from this package, unpredictable behavior will
  occur.

2.as1600 now accepts expressions in quantifiers.  Quantifiers are
  used to indicate repeated allocation of data or nop instructions.
  In the past, only constants could be used as quantifiers, as in:


  10 * nop            /* allocate 10 nops */
  20 * int            /* allocate space for 20 16-bit words */
   2 * int 0.1, 0.2, 0.3        /* allocate 2 reps of initialized */
                      /* data, as in 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3  */

  now a programmer could write:
  
  [5+5] * nop           /* allocate 10 nops */
  [2*10] * int          /* allocate space for 20 16-bit words */
  [4-2] * int 0.1, 0.2, 0.3  /* allocate 2 reps of initialized */
                   /* data, as in 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3  */
  
  
  
    IMPORTANT NOTE:  The square brackets (i.e., []) are a necessary
     part of the syntax!  You can still use a single constant without
     brackets, but each expression must be so enclosed.
  
    The assembler must be able to evaluate the expression at assembly
     time.  Relocatable expressions are not permitted.
  
    Of course, the programmer could have used preprocessor
     identifiers to make things more readable.  Here is an example:
  
    #define   np    10
    #define   nr    15
    #define   tot   np+nr
    #define   diff  [nr-np]
  
  
    label1:    [tot] * int   /* must use brackets here */
    label2:    diff * int    /* brackets are already in "diff" */


  3.The assembler now accepts the C++ type of comment.  Now two adjacent
     forward slashes will cause the remainder of a line to be ignored
     by as1600.  Programmers may mix the old C language style comments
     with the new C++ style comments.  Here is an example:

       start: mwait=0        // no wait states for external I/O
         pioc=0x1800    // set PIDS/PODS to active mode
       /*
         r0=ARRAY1 // point to input buffer
         r2=ARRAY2 // point to output buffer
       */
         a0=a0+p p=x*y /* x=*pt++ */ y=*r0++
       //a0=a0+p p=x*y /* x=*pt++ */ y=*r0++
       
      The combination of comments in the code fragment above
       illustrate the convenience of having both
      comment styles at the programmer's disposal. The programmer can
       now "comment out" blocks of code
      without having to eliminate the comments that accompany that
       code.
    
  4.   The j and k register can now be loaded with 256 without the
  addition of a "move" qualifier.  Previously, if a short-immediate
  load was used resulting in sign extension and the register was
  loaded with -256.

  5. x=y is now accepted as a destination for the 1604, 1616x11,
     1616x30, 1617, 1618, and 1630.  Provided the user has included
     the appropriate header file (e.g. 1604.h) in their source file.
     For example, the assembler will now accept the following syntax:
  
    auc=0x80
    x=y=a0
  
  
  The DSP1600 based devices mentioned above all have the capability of
  loading both the x and y registers from the same source in the same
  cycle.  This feature was added as a fast way to square values.  The
  programmer must be aware that the feature is turned on and off by setting
  and clearing bit 7 in the auc register.  Simply using the new syntax in
  the source file is not sufficient to load both registers from the same
  source.  This syntax has only been provided to allow programmers to
  express their intent in the source, and make the program more readable.
  They should be aware, however, that the assembler is incapable of
  performing static analysis to determine if their program is correct.

6.  The DSP1600 assembler now recognizes two new directives:
                .list off
                .list on
These directives turn the assembler listing on & off (if one was requestd).
They must be entered exactly as they appear above, since each is one
directive, and not separate tokens read by the parser.
The assembler listing will now contain everything it normally would except
for the text between the two directives.  The directives themselves will
never appear in an assembler listing.
        This feature spans #included files, so a header file (such as
1610.h) can be omitted from the listing by bracketing the #include with
.list off/.list on directives.
        If more than one assembler source file is assembled on a single
command line, the listing is reset to on at the beginning of each file.
In other words, each successive file produces a listing, until a .list off
directive is encountered in its own text.

7.    Customers have requested that the assembler screen out potential
errors relating to register sizes and immediates.  Certain registers, such
as c0, c1, and c2, are not 16 bits wide.  These three registers are actually
8 bits, signed.  In other words they should only be written with values
between -128 and 127.  In the latest release of the assembler, if the user
specifies -DSIZECHECK on the command line, as1600 will report warnings if
an immediate value exceeds a register's bit width.
        This has been accomplished by adding a new directive, .size, to 
the DSP1600 grammar.  The user doesn't need to specify these directives,
that is already done for them in the header files.  (As of this writing this
has not been done in the 1604.h and 1605.h header files.)
        Programmers should still be aware that the assembler only performs
16 bit arithmetic operations.  So there can still be problems if the assembler
is programmed with the following expression:
 
        r0 = 25000 * 3
 
        The register in question is 16 bits, and unsigned.  Therefore it can
hold any number between 0 and 65535.  This expression would evaluate to 75000
if it were done with 32 bits.  However, a 16 bit expression evaluator will lose
the high order bits, resulting in 9464.  The assembler would conclude that
this is a perfectly legitimate value for r0.
        A final reminder:  This feature only checks immediates!  That is to
say that the assembler is incapable of screening out a sequence of operations
such as:
 
        a0=0x7000
        c0=a0
 
Clearly c0 has been written with a number greater than eight bits, but since
as1600 can not be certain of the order of evaluation of its own instructions,
it can not identify this problem.
 
        The diagnostics generated by this feature are only warnings.  The
assembler will still create the same output file specifying 16 bit immediates
for the registers in question.  This leaves it to the programmer's discretion
whether or not to fix the problem.

9.    The assembler is not compatible with the make utility distributed
with Borland C/C++ 3.1.  This version of make runs in protected mode.  The
new assembler also runs in protected mode, which frees it from many of its
earlier memory limitations.  Unfortunately, the DOS extenders are not
compatible.  It is recommended that programmers use a make utility that does
not invoke a DOS extender on its own.

10. Previous releases of AS1600 used to display "Invalid DSP1610 opcode" as
    a result of certain errors.  It now reports an "Invalid DSP16xx opcode"
    instead.

11. Previous releases of AS1600 allowed the Z:R operation to specify any
    combination of rN and R.  This operation is not valid however when
    rN==R (eg: *r0zp : r0).  This is no longer allowed.

12. Previous releases of AS1600 would not accept a file that did not end
    in a newline character.  It would fail and report a "parse error".  It
    no longer fails in this case, instead acting as if a newline is present
    at the end of the file.
 




ld1600

1.   There are twelve linker information files included with this release of
the software tools.  Instructions on how to select ifiles are included in an
advisory entitled "DSP1600 Support Software Tools New Ifile Names" included
with this release.  See one of the DSP16XX Support Tools Manuals for details
on ifiles.

2.    When searching for a linker information file, ld1600 now uses the
LIB1600 environment variable.  If an information file specified on the command
line is not found in the current working directory, the linker will search the
directory pointed to by LIB1600 before reporting an error.

3.    In previous DOS releases of the SGS, ld1600 used to mirror any carriage
returns and EOF (end-of-file) characters it encountered in a linker information
file into the beginning of its output.  This could cause problems if the output
was redirected into a file, such as would be the case when creating a link map
file.  While extra carriage returns at the beginning of the file are usually
harmless, an EOF character causes many programs to stop reading the file. The
current release fixes this problem.

4.   ld1600 now displays the correct usage message when given an illegal
argument.  It now displays this message by default (rather than an
empty map) if no input files are specified.  This message is also
displayed (without any warning messages) if the "-?" option is given.
 
5.   ld1600 no longer aborts if "-oFILE" is specified (without spaces between
the option and the file name).  Instead it recognizes the file name as if it
had been typed "-o FILE".

6.   NEW FEATURE:
 
        A new section encoding feature has been provided.  This feature will
        automatically copy all of the data from one or more sections into
        another at final link time.  These sections are copied AFTER being
        relocated, so this only works for absolute linking ("-r" may not be
        specified).  A table is created which may be used to copy all of the
        data back into the original section.
 
        The main use for this feature is to allow code and data to be located
        in volatile memory and initialized at run-time from non-volatile
        memory.
 
        This feature is controlled through a new "ENCODE" section of the
        linker information file.  Its format is:
 
        ENCODE INTO targetSection USING symbolName {
                sourceSection1
                sourceSection2
                ...
        }
 
        This requests that the source sections listed be copied (after being
        relocated) into the end of the target section, followed by a table
        indicating where the copied data resides and to where it should be
        copied.  A symbol with the name given in the ENCODE section will be
        created so that the table may be referenced by the code.
 
        The table consists of a series of 3-word records, followed by a single
        zero word, as follows:
                length, original address, address of copy
                length, original address, address of copy
                ...
                0
 
        Each record corresponds to a single block of data which should be
        copied at run-time.  Each data block contains one or more sections
        that were relocated and copied by the linker.
 
        The section encoding feature is not actually enabled unless the "-i"
        (for initialized data) option is supplied when ld1600 is called.  If
        "-i" is not specified, the sections are not copied.  A table is still
        created as well as the symbol which points to it, but it will only
        contain a single zero word, indicating that it is empty.
 
7. Prior SGS releases contained a bug in LD1600 that occassionally caused
   erratic behavior when more than two or three options were passed to
   the linker (whether on the command line or through an ifile).

   Specifically, each option specified other than the first option used one
   of the 7 "slots" allocated for library directories that may be specified
   with the "-L" option (the "-L" option thus used two).  If the "-L" option
   was specified after the slots were all used, the error message "Too many
   -L options, 7 allowed" would be produced and LD1600 would abort; if not,
   no error message would be produced.  No other adverse effects have been
   reported, although the operation of LD1600 may have been affected by this
   condition.
 





list1600

This release of the SGS tools provides a listing utility called
list1600.  This differs from the existing assembler listing built into
as1600 in that it is a completely independent tool which may be used
on both DSP relocatable object files, and DSP executables produced by
the linker.  As a result, list1600 is able to provide users with a
comprehensive listing of every source file used to build their
application, complete with the relocated assembler labels.  In
addition, unlike the listing from as1600, list1600 uses the original
source code before the preprocessor was run.

The usage is as follows:

     list1600 [-vV] [-l<length>] <DSP1600 executable>
         -v:        verbose
         -V:        print the version number
         -l<length>:     accepts an integer indicating the line length

and finally, the DSP executable or object file is specified.

When the verbose option is used, list1600 prints the name of the
source files as it reads each one.  Later, it prints the name of the
source files as it writes the corresponding listing.  This feature was
added to allow the user to monitor list1600's progress, since it can
take several minutes to produces listings for large applications.  The
length option defaults to 80 columns.

All source files are expected to be in the current working directory
when list1600 is executed.  If any files are not found, list1600 will
look for them in the directory pointed to by the LIB1600 environment
variable.  If they are still not found, list1600 reports the error and
quits.

Instead of creating a single (possibly large) listing file, list1600
creates a subdirectory, called "listing", in the current working
directory.  In this directory it writes a separate listing file for
each of the source and header files used to assemble and link the
binary file.  This allows users to print or view areas of interest in
the listing, without having to sort through many pages of source code.

Here is an example of  list1600 output:


reloc.s                Page    2       Thu Jul  1 16:34:11 1993

addr:opcd       imm     line                         source
0035:5080       6f54      61         pt= 0x7000-exlabel  /*subtract extern*/
0037:5080       7f54      62         pt= 0x8000-exlabel
                          63
0039:30c0                 64         nop
003a:0039                 65     end:    goto end - 1     /*subtract local*/
003b:00ab                 66         goto exlabel-1
003c:80ab                 67         call exlabel-1
                          68         10 * int
                          69

In the page header, the fields are the name of the source file, the
page (pages are 60 lines long), and the modification date of the
source file, respectively.  As indicated by the heading above the
listing
itself, the fields in the listing are the address, opcode, immediate
(if any), line number, and original source code, respectively.  Since
this is the source code before preprocessing, all comments and
preprocessor macros are preserved.

As can be seen on line 68 of the example above, list1600 does not
produce address and code information for data allocation.  This is
unavoidable because line numbers are not generated for blocks of data
in the object files.

One final note:  list1600 can only produce a listing if the binary
file contains a symbol table and line number information.  In other
words, users should not use as1600 with the "-r" (no line numbers)
option nor should they strip out the symbol table with the ld1600 "-s"
option.  The executable or relocatable object file must be created as
one would create a file for use with one of the win16xx source code
debuggers.




head1600

To assist in adding the "#include 16xx.h" header line to source files
that do not already contain a header line, this release of the DSP1600
SGS includes a tool called head1600.  The command syntax is

     head1600 -hheader [-i][-b] file.s [file.s  . . .]

          where
          -hheader  specifies the name of the header file (i.e., 1616.h)
          -i        interactive (i.e., prompt for y/n)
          -b        save backup copies to file.old
          file.s         the name of the file to be modified.

For example, if source is to be generated for the DSP1616x30 device,
and assuming the file sio1.s is in the current directory, the command:

     $ head1600 -h1616x30.h -b sio1.s

will place

     #include <1616x30.h>

followed by a single blank line in the file sio1.s and also create a
file called sio1.old in the current directory.
  
  
  
mask1600


NOTE: For 3.1.1a and later SGS releases, mask1600 requires a "-s" argument to
specify the number of 1024 word pages of internal ROM.  Previously, mask1600
worked with 4096 word pages.

The mask1600 tool generates a ROM mask for the internal ROM on DSP1600 family
devices.  The usage is as follows:

usage: mask1600 [-V] -s<romsize> <DSP1600 executable program>

 	Where <romsize> specifies the number of 1K pages to use.
 	This will create 2 files:
 	(1) hex.out - the internal ROM code in hexadecimal, one word per line.
 		(save this file to verify the code AT&T returns.)
	(2) mask.out - the mask data for the ROM code.  Send this file to AT&T.

The DSP1600 executable program must be properly assembled and linked using a
ld1600 IFILE that specifies an internal ROM ".rom" section (usually memory map
1).  The mask1600 tool will issue an error message if the executable does not
contain a ".rom" section.  If the customer is submitting a secure ROM code, the
file crc16.v2 (also provided by AT&T) must be included.  Non-secure ROM codes
require the executable to be linked with the appropriate Hardware Development
System (HDS) monitor routine for the device.  A separate HDS monitor routine
file (16XXhds.v2 or 16XXhds.v3) is provided by AT&T on the device-specific tool
distribution.  

Two output files are generated by mask1600: hex.out and mask.out.  Send
mask.out to AT&T for encoding of  the ROM.  Keep hex.out for verification with
a file returned by AT&T.



prom1600


1.  The prom1600 utility in the 3.0 release of the SGS did not correctly
read all DSP files.  If it encountered the value 0x1A in a file, the output
file would be corrupted.  This was corrected.

2.  Previous releases of prom1600 had a problem which caused the addresses
in their output to wrap at 32K.  This has been corrected.



ar1600

The DSP1600 SGS Tools release now comes with an object file archive
utility on both DOS and UNIX.  The usage is as follows:

ar1600 {d|m|p|q|r|t|x} [a|b|i c l s u v V] [posname] archive files
 MODE OPTIONS
  d     delete named files
  m     move named files
  p     print named files
  q     quickly append named files
  r     replace named files
  t     list table of named|ALL files
  x     extract named|ALL files
 CONTROL OPTIONS
  a|b|i put named files after|before|before [posname] file; for r, m
  c     suppress archive creation message during r or q
  l     use local current working directory for temp files
  s     force regeneration of the archive symbol table (use with t)
  u     update mode for use with r
  v     verbose mode for use with t, x
  V     report ar1600's version and release number

The archiver creates library files which can then be used in conjunction
with the -l and -L options of the DSP1600 linker, ld1600.



cfig386

The DOS tools releases now include a utility to configure the DOS extender
stubs included in the executables.  This may be necessary when the tools are
installed in a directory that is not in the PATH, or when another set of DSP
tools is installed in a directory that is before them in the PATH.  Its usage
is as follows:

cfig386 -dosxname file_with_extender file_without_stub

The assembler and command-line simulator files contain a special DOS extender
that allows them to access memory above the normal limits of DOS.  The other
tools each contain a small stub that loads the DOS extender from one of these
files.  When the tools are first installed, the stubs are configured to access
the assembler or command-line simulator by searching the DOS PATH.  Normally,
this does not cause any problems.  However, if an earlier version of the
assembler or simulator is in the PATH before the intended one, this load will
fail.

If this problem occurs, it may be corrected by using cfig386 to encode the full
path name of the assembler or command-line simulator into the stub files.
Batch files have been provided with each of the tools to automate this process.
The file pfix1600.bat corrects the SGS files, and pfixXXXX.bat (where XXXX is
the chip name) corrects the simulator files.

None of these utilities should be used unless problems are experienced with the
tools.  Once the full path name of the executables is encoded into the stubs,
the tools cannot be moved to a different directory without changing the path
name using these utilities.



